-- Nexus: data/LocalizationPacks.lua
-- Optional Nexus presentation packs for core/Localization.lua (issue #123).
-- Data only: plain tables from a pack ID to the pack's own name (autonym)
-- and its entries, from a reviewed English key to text, with no function,
-- metatable or generated key. Placeholders, slot types, line policy and
-- result limits of formatted entries are owned by the code declarations in
-- core/Localization.lua, never by this data. core/Localization.lua loads
-- just after this file (Nexus.toc), checks every name and entry against the
-- same contract as the English base (a template against its schema), and
-- copies what it accepts once.
--
-- The pack IDs are the Nexus preference enum of data/DefaultProfile.lua
-- (uiLanguages). They are Nexus product choices, not game client locales,
-- GetLocale() results or aliases; the game's own language is never read or
-- changed.
--
-- Each pack holds one entry for every key of data/LocalizationEnglish.lua
-- (4078 keys), in the same order, and nothing else. A missing or invalid
-- entry still shows the exact English of its key at runtime; it never
-- falls back to an older translation.
--
-- All wording is draft (draft_unreviewed): drafts composed from the
-- per-language draft cohorts, each value taken unchanged from its selected
-- draft. The wording has had technical and source-paired automated review
-- and correction only; no competent human reviewer of any language has
-- accepted it, and no reviewer identity or date is recorded here. An entry
-- that is byte-identical to its English (a product, game or code-owned term,
-- or a placeholder-only text) is a draft choice. Entry-specific technical
-- review declarations, where one exists, are recorded separately in the
-- entryReview metadata of docs/localization/RELEASE_LANGUAGES.json; not every
-- such entry has one, and a declaration does not establish competent human,
-- native, rights or product-level language acceptance.
-- Client terminology, locale aliases, fonts, glyphs and layout fit are not
-- verified natively. Data presence alone does not prove that a consumer
-- shows a key or that a window is fully localized.

